+Localized Point Management (LPM) is a training optimization technique for 3D Gaussian Splatting that identifies error-contributing 3D zones via cross-view feature mapping and performs localized point addition, opacity reset, and pruning to correct under-optimized or ill-conditioned points. Unlike standard Adaptive Density Control (ADC) which relies on global gradient thresholds, LPM uses image rendering errors and multiview geometry constraints to target specific regions, improving reconstruction quality without the substantial training overhead of multi-view stereo or additional gradient propagation methods like GaussianPro or PixelGS.
 
 ### ParetoGS
-Decodes any Gaussian count from a single asset using sparse, adapted budget anchors and residual atlases [S1].
-
-#### Gaussian Densification via Rendering Loss
-A strategy to add new 3D Gaussians by identifying unobserved regions (holes) and high-error regions using opacity, color, and depth thresholds [S3]. It densifies regions where $O(x,y) < \tau_{opa}$, $E_{color}(x,y) > \tau_{color}$, or $E_{depth}(x,y)/D(x,y) > \tau_{depth}$ [S3].
-
-#### Point Densification
-A process in original 3DGS that adaptively controls the number of Gaussian points through clone and split operations to convert a sparse set into a denser one [S4]. In HO-Gaussian, this is performed at regular intervals to populate problematic regions [S4].
-
-#### Naive Fine-tuning
-A method identical to model rewriting except freely optimizing all weights of the generator [S5]. It is optimized over 10,000 steps with a learning rate of $10^{-3}$ [S5].
-
-#### Image-space Gaussian Fine-tuning
-A process to account for different visual modes by generating multiple radiance fields using different transfer functions and optimizing the Gaussian set against rendered images [S6, unofficial]. It selects 5 transfer functions per volume and renders 64 images per field for supervision [S6, unofficial].
+ParetoGS is a continuous-budget 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) representation that decodes any Gaussian count $K$ from a single asset without separate optimization per budget [S1]. It achieves this by constructing a small set of sparse, adapted budget anchors with persistent member identities, encoding their parameter changes relative to a full model in a residual atlas, and interpolating shared parameters while gradually activating entering members at runtime [S1]. Unlike prefix-based or hierarchical methods that require fixed orderings or complex transformation modules, ParetoGS explicitly optimizes nested anchors and connects their aligned states, offering superior quality at low budgets compared to FlexGS and NanoGS [S1].
+
+### [Multi-stage and Progressive Training](18485-multi-stage-and-progressive-training.md)
+Multi-stage and Progressive Training encompasses training protocols that decouple optimization into distinct phases or incremental steps to address specific challenges such as training instability, catastrophic forgetting, or the need for real-time scene updates. It solves the problem where simultaneous optimization of coupled parameters (e.g., geometry and quantization bottlenecks) or sequential data acquisition leads to convergence failure or overfitting. It sets itself apart from single-stage training by isolating foundational representation learning from attribute-specific refinement or by dynamically allocating resources based on image overlap hierarchies.
+
+### Gaussian Densification via Rendering Loss
+A strategy to add new 3D Gaussians to the map by identifying unobserved regions (holes) and regions with high rendering error, using opacity, color, and depth thresholds [S3]. It identifies holes where rendered opacity $O(x,y) < \tau_{opa}$ and high error regions where $E_{color}(x,y) > \tau_{color}$ or $E_{depth}(x,y)/D(x,y) > \tau_{depth}$ [S3]. For depth images, corresponding depths are projected directly [S3]. The opacity rendering is defined as $O = \sum_{i \in N} \alpha_i \prod_{j=1}^{i-1} (1 - \alpha_j)$ [S3].
+
+### Point Densification
+A process in original 3DGS that adaptively controls the number of Gaussian points through clone and split operations to convert a sparse set into a denser one [S4]. In HO-Gaussian, point densification is performed at regular intervals to provide new positions to the Gaussian pipeline, populating problematic regions [S4].
+
+### Naive Fine-tuning
+Naive generator fine-tuning method identical to model rewriting except freely optimizing all weights of the generator [S5]. It is optimized over 10,000 steps with a learning rate of $10^{-3}$ [S5].
+
+### Image-space Gaussian Fine-tuning
+A fine-tuning process to account for different visual modes in the volume by generating multiple radiance fields using different transfer functions and optimizing the Gaussian set against rendered images [S6, unofficial]. It selects a series of transfer functions $\{TF_h\}$, generates radiance fields $RF_h = TF_h(F)$, renders 64 images per field for supervision, and backpropagates image-space loss to update the Gaussian set $G_h$ [S6, unofficial]. In practice, 5 transfer functions are selected for each volume [S6, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of Gaussians $N$ and views $V$. Rendering is $O(N)$ per pixel. Optimization is dominated by backpropagation through the rasterizer. Fine-tuning iterations (e.g., 10,000) can take hours to days depending on scene size and hardware [S1, S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing Gaussian attributes. Pruning reduces this [S2].
-*   **Performance:** Fine-tuning with pretrained priors accelerates convergence compared to test-time optimization [S2]. Rendering-Guided Densification improves map quality in SLAM [S3].
-*   **Strengths:** High fidelity, real-time rendering, explicit representation [S2, S3].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of densification thresholds; prone to forgetting in continuous mapping [S3]; sparse initialization can lead to holes [S4].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Fine-tuning iterations are typically fewer than initial training if a good initialization is provided, but dynamic densification adds overhead per step [S2]. Naive fine-tuning may require up to 10,000 steps [S5].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the number of Gaussians. Dynamic densification increases memory usage, while pruning reduces it [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Improves reconstruction quality, handles sparse or noisy inputs, adapts to specific domains (e.g., street scenes, blurry images) [S1, S2, S3].
+*   **Limitations:** Can overfit to input views if not regularized [S3]. Requires careful tuning of hyperparameters (learning rate, thresholds) [S5].
+*   **When to Use:** When initial reconstruction is poor, when dealing with sparse views, or when adapting a pre-trained model to a new domain [S1, S2].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When computational resources are extremely limited and a coarse approximation is sufficient, or when the initial initialization is already optimal.
 
 ## Background
-Fine-tuning in 3DGS builds on the original 3D Gaussian Splatting method by Kerbl et al. [9] (cited in [S3]), which introduced adaptive density control. The concept of fine-tuning pretrained models is common in CNNs (e.g., VGGNet, AlexNet) [S1]. In the context of 3DGS, it has been adapted to refine feed-forward predictions [S2], handle sparse views [S1], and improve SLAM [S3].
+Fine-tuning is a general machine learning technique adapted for 3D Gaussian Splatting. In the context of 3DGS, it was introduced to leverage pre-trained priors (e.g., from ImageNet or other 3D datasets) to accelerate convergence and improve generalization [S2, S5]. The specific application to Gaussian Splatting involves optimizing the explicit Gaussian representation after an initial sparse or feed-forward initialization [S2]. The method has evolved to include dynamic densification strategies to handle the non-uniform density of 3D scenes [S3, S4].
 
